## Problem

The code emitter generates assembly text for every instruction, even when `--emit asm` is not requested:

```rust
// crates/rue-codegen/src/x86_64/emit.rs
fn end_inst(&mut self) {
    let bytes = self.code[self.inst_start..].to_vec();  // Always allocates
    // Assembly text is always generated via format!()
}
```

This causes unnecessary string formatting and allocation during normal compilation.

## Proposed Solution

Add a flag to control assembly text generation:

```rust
struct Emitter {
    emit_asm: bool,
    // ...
}

fn end_inst(&mut self) {
    if self.emit_asm {
        let asm = format!(...);
        self.instructions.push(EmittedInst { bytes, asm: Some(asm) });
    } else {
        // Skip allocation entirely for code-only path
    }
}
```

## Impact

- Eliminates string formatting/allocation overhead during normal compilation
- Only pay the cost when `--emit asm` is actually used
- Low priority since emission is typically fast, but good hygiene

