## Problem

Warnings are collected in a shared `Vec<CompileWarning>` during Sema:

```rust
// crates/rue-air/src/sema.rs
pub struct Sema<'a> {
    warnings: Vec<CompileWarning>,  // Shared mutable\!
    ...
}
```

This blocks parallel function analysis - multiple threads would race on the warnings vector.

## Proposed Solution

Return warnings per-function, merge after parallel phase:

```rust
/// Each function analysis returns its own warnings
fn analyze_function_body(
    ctx: &TypeContext,
    fn_view: RirFunctionView<'_>,
) -> CompileResult<(AnalyzedFunction, Vec<CompileWarning>)> {
    let mut local_warnings = Vec::new();
    // ... analysis, push to local_warnings ...
    Ok((analyzed, local_warnings))
}

/// Parallel driver merges warnings
fn analyze_all_parallel(...) -> CompileResult<SemaOutput> {
    let results: Vec<_> = functions
        .par_iter()
        .map(|f| analyze_function_body(ctx, f))
        .collect::<Result<Vec<_>, _>>()?;

    // Merge warnings from all functions
    let warnings: Vec<_> = results
        .iter()
        .flat_map(|(_, w)| w.iter().cloned())
        .collect();

    // Sort by source location for consistent output
    warnings.sort_by_key(|w| w.span().map(|s| s.start));

    Ok(SemaOutput { warnings, ... })
}
```

## Alternative: Thread-Safe Collection

```rust
use parking_lot::Mutex;

struct ParallelWarnings(Mutex<Vec<CompileWarning>>);

impl ParallelWarnings {
    fn push(&self, w: CompileWarning) {
        self.0.lock().push(w);
    }
}
```

The per-function approach is preferred - less contention, cleaner code.

